Amazon S3

Best practice: Follow the principle of least privilege.

Avoid using permanent AWS access keys in your application when an appropriate IAM role can provide the required access.
Q16. Your application needs to connect to a database, but you don't want database credentials exposed in the source code. What would you do?
Interview answer:

"I would store sensitive credentials in AWS Secrets Manager or another appropriate secrets-management service instead of hardcoding them in the source code.

I would grant the application access through an IAM role and retrieve the secrets securely. I would also configure credential rotation where appropriate and ensure secrets are not written to application logs."

